{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1805 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "f52fb9e97417ed8c67b21918009398e057714dac07e06ed40e62e195fb407492" :edb/db-before-t 1804 :edb/db-after-t 1805 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535117 50 #inst "2026-10-08T17:17:20.830Z" 13194139535117 true] [17592186216940 1100 "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n- $y -xy' -\\ln(y')=0$\n\n- $y^4\\cos(x) +xyy' = y^2$\n\n- $y= xy' + 2y'$" 13194139535117 true] [17592186216940 1100 "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n1. $y -xy' -\\ln(y')=0$\n2. $y^4\\cos(x) +xyy' = y^2$\n3. $y= xy' + 2y'$" 13194139535117 false] [17592186216940 1145 "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny = xy' + g(y')\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of $y'$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y$, we get\n$y = xy' + \\ln(y')$,\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y = xy' + g(y')$ with\n$g(y') = \\ln(y')$.\n- Equation II is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Dividing the equation by $y$, we get\n$y^3\\cos(x) +xy' = y$.\nThis equation cannot be written in the form $y = xy' + g(y')$.\n- Equation III is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y = xy' + g(y')$ with\n$g(y') = 2y'$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535117 false] [17592186216940 1145 "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny = xy' + g(y'),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of $y'.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny = xy' + \\ln(y'),\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y = xy' + g(y')$ with\n\n$$\ng(y') = \\ln(y').\n$$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Dividing the equation by $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny^3\\cos(x) +xy' = y.\n$$\n\nThis equation cannot be written in the form $y = xy' + g(y').$\n\n- Equation III is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y = xy' + g(y')$ with \n\n$$\ng(y') = 2y'.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535117 true] [17592186216941 1100 "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny = xy' + (y')^2+1.\n$$" 13194139535117 true] [17592186216941 1100 "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n$y = xy' + (y')^2+1$." 13194139535117 false] [17592186216941 1145 "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny = xy' + g(y')\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of $y'$.\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny = xy' + (y')^2 +1\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p = y'$.\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny = xp + p^2+1\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= (xp + p^{2} + 1)^{′} \\\\\np &= p + xp^{′} + 2pp^{′} \\\\\n0 &= p^{′}(x + 2p)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if\n\n$$\nx + 2p = 0\n$$\n\nThen,\n$x = -2p$.\n\nNow, let's substitute this into\n\n$$\ny = xp + p^2+1{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= xp + p^{2} + 1 \\\\\n&= (-2p)p + p^{2} + 1 \\\\\n&=-2p^{2} + p^{2} + 1 \\\\\n&=-p^{2} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n{x =-2p; y =-p^{2} + 1\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p$, we first solve the first equation for $p{:}$\n\n$$\np = -\\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -p^{2} + 1 \\\\\n&= - (-\\frac{x}{2})^{2} + 1 \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{x^{2}}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny = 1- \\dfrac{x^2}{4}\n$$" 13194139535117 false] [17592186216941 1145 "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny = xy' + g(y'),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of $y'.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny = xy' + (y')^2 +1,\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p = y'.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny = xp + p^2+1.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+{p}^{2}+1)^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+2p{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+2p)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x + 2p = 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx = -2p.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y = xp + p^2+1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+{p}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =(-2p)p+{p}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =-2{p}^{2}+{p}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =-{p}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-2p \\\\ y=-{p}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $p{:}$\n\n$$\np = -\\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{p}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =-{(-\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny = 1- \\dfrac{x^2}{4}.\n$$" 13194139535117 true] [17592186216944 1100 "Find the general solution of the Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny = xy' + e^{-y'}\n$$" 13194139535117 true] [17592186216944 1100 "Find the general solution of the Clairaut differential equation:\n$y = xy' + e^{-y'}$" 13194139535117 false] [17592186216944 1145 "To solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny = xy' + e^{-y'}\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p = y'$.\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny = x p + e^{-p}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= (xp + e^{-p})^{′} \\\\\np &= p + xp^{′} - e^{-p}p^{′} \\\\\n0 &= p^{′}(x - e^{-p})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, which means at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nThe general solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $p' = 0$. Then, $p$ is constant, say\n$p = c$.\n\nSubstituting $p = c$ into\n\n$$\ny = x p + e^{-p}\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution\n\n$$\ny = cx + e^{-c}\n$$\n\nThis represents a family of straight lines, one line for each constant value of $c$." 13194139535117 false] [17592186216944 1145 "To solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny = xy' + e^{-y'},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p = y'.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny = x p + e^{-p}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+{e}^{-p})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}-{e}^{-p}{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x-{e}^{-p})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, which means at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nThe general solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $p' = 0.$ Then, $p$ is constant, say \n\n$$\np = c.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $p = c$ into $y = x p + e^{-p},$ we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny = cx + e^{-c}\n$$\n\nThis represents a family of straight lines, one line for each constant value of $c.$" 13194139535117 true] [17592186217190 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332081 13194139535117 true] [17592186217190 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332088 13194139535117 true] [17592186217190 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332095 13194139535117 true] [17592186217190 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332102 13194139535117 true] [17592186217191 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332109 13194139535117 true] [17592186217191 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332116 13194139535117 true] [17592186217191 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332123 13194139535117 true] [17592186217191 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332130 13194139535117 true] [17592186217192 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332137 13194139535117 true] [17592186217192 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332144 13194139535117 true] [17592186217192 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332151 13194139535117 true] [17592186217724 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535117 true] [17592186217724 1100 "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n- $y=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′})^{3}$\n- $y{y}^{′}-x{y}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)y$\n- $x{y}^{′}+{e}^{{y}^{′}}-y=0$" 13194139535117 true] [17592186217724 1100 "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n1. $y = xy^{′} + (y^{′})^{3}$\n2. $yy^{′} - xy^{3} + 2xy^{2} = \\sin (x)y$\n3. $xy^{′} + e^{y^{′}} - y = 0$" 13194139535117 false] [17592186217724 1145 "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})=({y}^{′})^{3}.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Dividing the equation by $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-x{y}^{2}+2xy=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nThis equation cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{e}^{{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})={e}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535117 true] [17592186217725 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535117 true] [17592186217725 1100 "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{e}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$" 13194139535117 true] [17592186217725 1100 "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$y = xy^{′} + e^{y^{′}}$." 13194139535117 false] [17592186217725 1145 "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{e}^{{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+{e}^{p}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+{e}^{p})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+{p}^{′}{e}^{p} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+{e}^{p})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x+{e}^{p}=0.$ Then, \n\n$$\nx=-{e}^{p}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp+{e}^{p}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+{e}^{p} \\\\  & =(-{e}^{p})p+{e}^{p} \\\\  & =(1-p){e}^{p}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-{e}^{p} \\\\ y=(1-p){e}^{p}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $p:$\n\n$$\np=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x)\n$$\n\nSince $x=-{e}^{p}$ and ${e}^{p}>0,$ we must have $x<0.$ Thus, $p=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x)$ is valid only for $x<0.$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(1-p){e}^{p} \\\\  & =(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x)){e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x)} \\\\  & =x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x)-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x)-1),\\,x<0.\n$$" 13194139535117 true] [17592186217727 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535117 true] [17592186217727 1100 "Find the general solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′})^{2}.\n$$" 13194139535117 true] [17592186217727 1100 "Find the general solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$y = xy^{′} + (y^{′})^{2}$." 13194139535117 false] [17592186217727 1145 "To solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′})^{2},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+{p}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+{p}^{2})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+2p{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+2p)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, which means at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nThe general solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if ${p}^{′}=0.$ Then, $p$ is constant, say \n\n$$\np=c.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $p=c$ into $y=xp+{p}^{2},$ we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=cx+{c}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a family of straight lines, one line for each constant value of $c.$" 13194139535117 true] [17592186217743 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535117 true] [17592186217743 1100 "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′})^{2}.\n$$" 13194139535117 true] [17592186217743 1100 "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$y = xy^{′} + (y^{′})^{2}$." 13194139535117 false] [17592186217743 1145 "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′})^{2},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+{p}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+{p}^{2})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+2p{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+2p)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x+2p=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx=-2p.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp+{p}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =(-2p)p+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =-2{p}^{2}+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =-{p}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-2p \\\\ y=-{p}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $p:$\n\n$$\np=-\\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{p}^{2} \\\\  & =-{(-\\frac{x}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}.\n$$" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332081 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332082 13194139535117 true] [17592186332081 1089 "q-334000" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332081 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535117 true] [17592186332081 1100 "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n- $y={x}^{3}{y}^{′}+x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)$\n- $x{y}^{4}-xy{y}^{′}-{y}^{2}=0$\n- $y=x{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}$" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332081 1113 #uuid "97007657-3a43-530e-8e1c-1dfd8246c0ca" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332081 1145 "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. In fact, it cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Dividing the equation by $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx{y}^{3}-x{y}^{′}-y=0.\n$$\n\nAgain, this equation cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})=\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332082 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332083 13194139535117 true] [17592186332082 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332084 13194139535117 true] [17592186332082 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332085 13194139535117 true] [17592186332082 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332086 13194139535117 true] [17592186332082 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332087 13194139535117 true] [17592186332082 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535117 true] [17592186332082 1041 "selection" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332082 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332085 13194139535117 true] [17592186332082 1110 #uuid "2963609a-310d-519d-b5b0-ca99ea28b833" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332083 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332083 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535117 true] [17592186332083 1111 #uuid "03fec8ac-3611-57c7-a479-fb76f9e00cf1" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332084 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332084 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535117 true] [17592186332084 1111 #uuid "9f4f39a8-b1fb-5ee1-a94f-9563971ab8a7" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332085 1034 "III only" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332085 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535117 true] [17592186332085 1111 #uuid "1a051469-73a6-5a14-a502-84d108b9a1be" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332086 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332086 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535117 true] [17592186332086 1111 #uuid "6b0e341a-5a36-57d8-a5aa-7ccfef0d4d74" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332087 1034 "II only" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332087 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535117 true] [17592186332087 1111 #uuid "06615766-a1ab-5c84-a233-59e916a01d2c" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332088 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332089 13194139535117 true] [17592186332088 1089 "q-334133" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332088 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535117 true] [17592186332088 1100 "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n- $y+2=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}$\n- ${y}^{′}-y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{′})$\n- $x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}-y=0$" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332088 1113 #uuid "17c86934-9bcf-5735-a8b5-c941974ac22b" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332088 1145 "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}-2,\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})={y}^{′}-2.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny={y}^{′}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nThis equation cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′},\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})={y}^{′}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332089 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332090 13194139535117 true] [17592186332089 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332091 13194139535117 true] [17592186332089 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332092 13194139535117 true] [17592186332089 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332093 13194139535117 true] [17592186332089 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332094 13194139535117 true] [17592186332089 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535117 true] [17592186332089 1041 "selection" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332089 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332091 13194139535117 true] [17592186332089 1110 #uuid "5367a1bf-7fac-5198-8293-389cb8e50f39" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332090 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332090 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535117 true] [17592186332090 1111 #uuid "c35c24d0-d7fe-5d15-825c-d76679c88b47" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332091 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332091 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535117 true] [17592186332091 1111 #uuid "2b1a441b-1287-57b3-a284-7e22fa166395" 13194139535117 true] [175921863