$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+2{p}^{2}+3)^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+4p{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+4p)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x+4p=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx=-4p.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp+2{p}^{2}+3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+2{p}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =(-4p)p+2{p}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =-4{p}^{2}+2{p}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =-2{p}^{2}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-4p \\\\ y=-2{p}^{2}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $p:$ \n\n$$\np=-\\frac{x}{4}.\n$$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2{p}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =-2{(-\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=3-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}.\n$$" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332152 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332153 13194139535117 true] [17592186332152 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332154 13194139535117 true] [17592186332152 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332155 13194139535117 true] [17592186332152 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332156 13194139535117 true] [17592186332152 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332157 13194139535117 true] [17592186332152 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535117 true] [17592186332152 1041 "selection" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332152 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332157 13194139535117 true] [17592186332152 1110 #uuid "78950484-4e7a-53ff-8cf3-8d22f49a0ee6" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332153 1034 "y=3+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332153 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535117 true] [17592186332153 1111 #uuid "cd4656f3-e415-582f-835d-66def5357205" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332154 1034 "y=3-{x}^{2}" 13194139535117 true] [17592186332154 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535117 true] [17592